WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.810
Welcome to Module 0: Crypto From Zero.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.288
If you've never owned crypto, never opened a

00:00:06.288 --> 00:00:08.491
wallet, and you're not even sure what a

00:00:08.491 --> 00:00:11.490
blockchain is, you're in exactly the right place.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:13.175
This module was built for you.

00:00:14.475 --> 00:00:16.214
Over the next 20 minutes or so,

00:00:16.214 --> 00:00:18.145
I'm going to tell you a story.

00:00:18.145 --> 00:00:19.960
It's about someone called Sam.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.338
Sam is a lot like most

00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:23.375
people who join this program.

00:00:23.375 --> 00:00:25.895
Curious, a little nervous, and completely new.

00:00:27.195 --> 00:00:29.245
Sam had heard the words everywhere.

00:00:29.245 --> 00:00:30.840
Bitcoin, on the news.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.895
Wallets and seed phrases, from a friend at work.

00:00:33.895 --> 00:00:36.560
DeFi, in a video that promised the moon.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:38.165
And a story about someone who

00:00:38.165 --> 00:00:40.445
lost everything with one wrong click.

00:00:40.445 --> 00:00:42.854
It all sounded exciting, confusing and

00:00:42.854 --> 00:00:44.645
a bit dangerous, all at once.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.175
So here's what this video will do.

00:00:48.175 --> 00:00:51.042
First, you'll understand what crypto actually

00:00:51.042 --> 00:00:53.710
is, using pictures from everyday life.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.638
Second, you'll see the tools Sam

00:00:55.638 --> 00:00:57.690
used, and why each one exists.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.689
Third, you'll learn the handful of mistakes that cause

00:01:00.689 --> 00:01:03.715
almost every beginner loss, and how to avoid them.

00:01:03.715 --> 00:01:06.291
And fourth, you'll see exactly how Module

00:01:06.291 --> 00:01:08.192
0 walks you through all of it,

00:01:08.192 --> 00:01:09.725
one small step at a time.

00:01:11.025 --> 00:01:12.145
Chapter 1.

00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:13.305
Money is a record.

00:01:14.605 --> 00:01:16.635
Sam's first surprise was this.

00:01:16.635 --> 00:01:19.170
Most of the money in your life isn't cash.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.815
It's a number in a list.

00:01:20.815 --> 00:01:22.225
When you get paid, no one

00:01:22.225 --> 00:01:23.990
carries notes to your bank.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.980
Someone changes a number in the bank's

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.970
records, and that number is your money.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.465
Picture a village with one notebook.

00:01:31.465 --> 00:01:33.451
Every time someone pays someone else,

00:01:33.451 --> 00:01:35.475
the village clerk writes it down.

00:01:35.475 --> 00:01:37.405
Ana paid Ben 10 coins.

00:01:37.405 --> 00:01:39.075
Ben paid Cai 5.

00:01:39.075 --> 00:01:42.855
Everyone trusts the notebook, because everyone trusts the clerk.

00:01:42.855 --> 00:01:44.550
That notebook has a name.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:45.705
It's called a ledger.

00:01:47.005 --> 00:01:48.670
Your bank is that clerk.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.375
It keeps the record for you.

00:01:50.375 --> 00:01:53.245
It can reverse a payment if something goes wrong.

00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:55.360
It can help when you forget a password.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.207
But it can also freeze your account,

00:01:57.207 --> 00:01:59.305
and you have to trust it completely.

00:01:59.305 --> 00:02:01.585
Crypto changes who's in charge.

00:02:01.585 --> 00:02:04.073
In your own wallet, the network keeps the

00:02:04.073 --> 00:02:06.502
record, no one can reverse a payment, no

00:02:06.502 --> 00:02:08.872
one can reset your password, and no one

00:02:08.872 --> 00:02:10.175
can freeze it but you.

00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:12.936
So what if the village didn't

00:02:12.936 --> 00:02:14.330
need a clerk at all?

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.667
What if, instead of one notebook, every

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:19.121
villager kept an identical copy, and they

00:02:19.121 --> 00:02:21.645
all checked each new entry together?

00:02:21.645 --> 00:02:24.040
That's the idea behind a blockchain.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.495
It's a shared ledger.

00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:28.113
Thousands of computers keep the same copy,

00:02:28.113 --> 00:02:30.910
and old entries can't be quietly changed.

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.495
The trade-off is simple, and

00:02:32.495 --> 00:02:34.025
it matters: there's no undo.

00:02:35.325 --> 00:02:37.310
Here's why nobody can cheat.

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.757
Imagine someone edits their copy to

00:02:39.757 --> 00:02:41.960
say they received 100 coins.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.060
Every other copy disagrees, so the

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.640
network simply ignores the edited one.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.534
To fake a payment, you'd have to overpower most

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:53.090
of the network at once, which is enormously expensive.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.012
No single company is in charge, and that's also

00:02:56.012 --> 00:02:58.325
why no one can undo a payment for you.

00:02:59.625 --> 00:03:01.250
And why the word chain?

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:04.558
New entries are grouped into pages, called blocks.

00:03:04.558 --> 00:03:06.634
Each block carries a kind of seal, a

00:03:06.634 --> 00:03:09.607
fingerprint made from everything written on it, plus

00:03:09.607 --> 00:03:11.478
the seal of the page before.

00:03:11.478 --> 00:03:13.163
So the pages are linked.

00:03:13.163 --> 00:03:16.057
Change one old entry, and its seal changes,

00:03:16.057 --> 00:03:18.923
and every page after it stops matching.

00:03:18.923 --> 00:03:20.262
That's why history on a

00:03:20.262 --> 00:03:22.383
blockchain can't be quietly rewritten.

00:03:23.683 --> 00:03:25.318
So, in one picture.

00:03:25.318 --> 00:03:28.960
Bitcoin, launched in 2009, was the first blockchain:

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.078
digital money with no bank in the middle.

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:35.898
Ethereum, launched in 2015, went further.

00:03:35.898 --> 00:03:38.035
It lets people put small programs on

00:03:38.035 --> 00:03:40.404
the blockchain, which is what makes apps

00:03:40.404 --> 00:03:42.668
like lending and trading possible.

00:03:42.668 --> 00:03:45.103
Most of this program happens on Ethereum

00:03:45.103 --> 00:03:47.063
and the networks built around it.

00:03:48.363 --> 00:03:50.428
Sam asked the obvious question.

00:03:50.428 --> 00:03:52.253
Why would anyone want this?

00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:53.468
Four reasons.

00:03:53.468 --> 00:03:55.953
You can send money worldwide, to anyone,

00:03:55.953 --> 00:03:58.072
at any time, often in minutes.

00:03:58.072 --> 00:04:00.907
You can hold it yourself, with no bank needed.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.012
The money is programmable, so apps

00:04:03.012 --> 00:04:05.597
can lend, trade and pay automatically.

00:04:05.597 --> 00:04:06.938
And the rules are open:

00:04:06.938 --> 00:04:08.802
anyone can check the record.

00:04:08.802 --> 00:04:10.797
But notice the line at the bottom.

00:04:10.797 --> 00:04:12.204
The same things that make

00:04:12.204 --> 00:04:14.452
it powerful make mistakes permanent.

00:04:14.452 --> 00:04:16.067
That's why we start with safety.

00:04:17.368 --> 00:04:18.558
Chapter 2.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:19.443
The front door.

00:04:20.743 --> 00:04:23.433
Sam's first practical question was simple.

00:04:23.433 --> 00:04:25.408
How do I actually get some?

00:04:25.408 --> 00:04:28.538
The answer, for almost everyone, is an exchange.

00:04:28.538 --> 00:04:30.014
Think of it as a currency

00:04:30.014 --> 00:04:31.903
exchange desk at an airport.

00:04:31.903 --> 00:04:33.844
You hand over dollars, pounds or

00:04:33.844 --> 00:04:35.668
euros, and you get crypto back.

00:04:36.968 --> 00:04:38.666
Before Sam deposited a single

00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:40.608
dollar, they locked the door.

00:04:40.608 --> 00:04:42.436
First, the account: a unique

00:04:42.436 --> 00:04:44.563
email and a password manager.

00:04:44.563 --> 00:04:48.713
Second, two-factor authentication, using an authenticator app,

00:04:48.713 --> 00:04:51.713
not text messages, which can be hijacked.

00:04:51.713 --> 00:04:54.352
Third, a withdrawal whitelist, so money can

00:04:54.352 --> 00:04:57.111
only leave to addresses Sam has approved.

00:04:57.111 --> 00:05:00.043
And fourth, an anti-phishing code, a word that

00:05:00.043 --> 00:05:03.213
appears in every real email from the exchange.

00:05:03.213 --> 00:05:04.788
Fake emails won't have it.

00:05:06.088 --> 00:05:07.591
Then Sam made a small first

00:05:07.591 --> 00:05:09.663
buy, and learned the next lesson.

00:05:09.663 --> 00:05:10.928
The price on the chart

00:05:10.928 --> 00:05:12.328
is not what you get.

00:05:12.328 --> 00:05:14.198
There's the spread, the gap between

00:05:14.198 --> 00:05:16.005
the buying and selling price.

00:05:16.005 --> 00:05:17.866
There's a trading fee, sometimes

00:05:17.866 --> 00:05:19.745
hidden as a convenience fee.

00:05:19.745 --> 00:05:21.075
And there's a network cost

00:05:21.075 --> 00:05:22.410
when you move it out.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.305
What matters is what actually lands in your wallet.

00:05:25.305 --> 00:05:28.329
Compare offers by that, not by the headline price.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:32.444
Sam also met something called a stablecoin.

00:05:32.444 --> 00:05:35.049
Think of it as a digital dollar voucher.

00:05:35.049 --> 00:05:37.979
For each token, the issuer holds about a dollar

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:41.454
in reserve, in cash and short-term government bonds.

00:05:41.454 --> 00:05:43.802
The token moves on the blockchain, any

00:05:43.802 --> 00:05:46.209
time of day, like any other crypto.

00:05:46.209 --> 00:05:48.334
And you can swap it back for a dollar.

00:05:48.334 --> 00:05:50.994
It's how people hold steady value on-chain.

00:05:50.994 --> 00:05:52.525
But it's only as good as the

00:05:52.525 --> 00:05:54.619
reserves and the issuer behind it.

00:05:54.619 --> 00:05:56.144
It can still fail, and you'll

00:05:56.144 --> 00:05:57.669
learn how to judge that later.

00:05:58.969 --> 00:06:02.444
So Sam's first-buy plan was deliberately boring.

00:06:02.444 --> 00:06:04.043
A small learning amount, money

00:06:04.043 --> 00:06:05.684
they could afford to lose.

00:06:05.684 --> 00:06:07.432
A little ETH, the coin

00:06:07.432 --> 00:06:09.784
that pays fees on Ethereum.

00:06:09.784 --> 00:06:13.479
A little USDC, a widely used stablecoin.

00:06:13.479 --> 00:06:16.064
And the cheapest route, checked before clicking buy.

00:06:17.364 --> 00:06:18.564
Chapter 3.

00:06:18.564 --> 00:06:19.739
Who holds the keys?

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:22.789
Here's the idea that changed how

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:24.574
Sam thought about all of it.

00:06:24.574 --> 00:06:26.184
When your crypto sits on an

00:06:26.184 --> 00:06:28.669
exchange, the exchange holds the keys.

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:31.727
It's convenient for buying, selling and cashing out,

00:06:31.727 --> 00:06:34.553
but you're trusting a company, and companies can

00:06:34.553 --> 00:06:37.161
freeze accounts, get hacked, or go bust.

00:06:37.161 --> 00:06:39.453
In your own wallet, you hold the keys.

00:06:39.453 --> 00:06:41.078
Nobody can freeze it.

00:06:41.078 --> 00:06:42.469
But if you lose the keys,

00:06:42.469 --> 00:06:43.913
nobody can help you either.

00:06:45.213 --> 00:06:47.138
So what is a wallet, really?

00:06:47.138 --> 00:06:49.133
Picture a row of mailboxes.

00:06:49.133 --> 00:06:51.797
Your address is the slot on your mailbox.

00:06:51.797 --> 00:06:53.451
You can share it freely, and

00:06:53.451 --> 00:06:55.177
anyone can drop coins in.

00:06:55.177 --> 00:06:57.019
Your private key is the only

00:06:57.019 --> 00:06:58.779
key that opens the box.

00:06:58.779 --> 00:07:00.268
Whoever has it can empty the

00:07:00.268 --> 00:07:01.954
box, so you never share it.

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:04.478
And your seed phrase is the master

00:07:04.478 --> 00:07:06.785
recipe: 12 or 24 words that can

00:07:06.785 --> 00:07:09.104
recreate every key you have.

00:07:09.104 --> 00:07:10.333
It's your backup if a

00:07:10.333 --> 00:07:11.674
phone is lost or broken.

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:15.968
Here's a detail that surprises most beginners.

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:19.043
Your coins aren't actually inside the wallet app.

00:07:19.043 --> 00:07:20.798
They live on the blockchain.

00:07:20.798 --> 00:07:22.368
The wallet just holds the keys

00:07:22.368 --> 00:07:23.833
that let you move them.

00:07:23.833 --> 00:07:25.924
That's why a seed phrase can restore

00:07:25.924 --> 00:07:27.733
everything on a brand new phone.

00:07:29.033 --> 00:07:31.383
This is what a seed phrase looks like.

00:07:31.383 --> 00:07:34.028
These are example words, never use them.

00:07:34.028 --> 00:07:37.808
The words come from a standard list of 2,048.

00:07:37.808 --> 00:07:40.097
Whoever has these words, in this order,

00:07:40.097 --> 00:07:42.343
controls every coin in the wallet.

00:07:42.343 --> 00:07:45.104
So they're written on paper or stamped in metal,

00:07:45.104 --> 00:07:48.203
and never typed, photographed, stored online or shared.

00:07:49.503 --> 00:07:52.556
Sam set up their wallet from the official website,

00:07:52.556 --> 00:07:55.370
wrote the words on paper, in order, and stored

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.783
them somewhere private and safe from fire and water.

00:07:58.783 --> 00:08:01.248
Then Sam did the step most people skip.

00:08:01.248 --> 00:08:04.252
They tested a restore, before depositing anything,

00:08:04.252 --> 00:08:06.613
to prove the backup actually works.

00:08:06.613 --> 00:08:09.056
And they learned the don'ts: never type it

00:08:09.056 --> 00:08:11.840
into a website, never photograph it, never share

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:13.942
it, and never use a phrase that came

00:08:13.942 --> 00:08:15.817
pre-printed or from someone else.

00:08:17.117 --> 00:08:18.552
And here's the rule that will

00:08:18.552 --> 00:08:20.382
protect you more than any other.

00:08:20.382 --> 00:08:23.338
No real support team, no exchange, no wallet

00:08:23.338 --> 00:08:26.491
company, and nobody from this program will ever

00:08:26.491 --> 00:08:28.959
ask for your seed phrase or keys.

00:08:28.959 --> 00:08:31.299
Anyone who asks is trying to rob you.

00:08:31.299 --> 00:08:32.124
Every time.

00:08:33.424 --> 00:08:34.514
Quick check.

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:37.434
A friendly support agent messages Sam and asks

00:08:37.434 --> 00:08:40.094
for the seed phrase, to fix a problem.

00:08:40.094 --> 00:08:41.139
What should Sam do?

00:08:45.559 --> 00:08:47.464
The answer: never share it.

00:08:47.464 --> 00:08:49.429
Real support never asks.

00:08:49.429 --> 00:08:51.049
Close the chat and report it.

00:08:52.349 --> 00:08:53.559
Chapter 4.

00:08:53.559 --> 00:08:54.679
Roads and stamps.

00:08:55.979 --> 00:08:57.957
Now Sam wanted to move crypto from

00:08:57.957 --> 00:09:00.234
the exchange into their own wallet.

00:09:00.234 --> 00:09:02.009
That's when they met networks.

00:09:02.009 --> 00:09:04.204
Think of networks as separate roads.

00:09:04.204 --> 00:09:06.537
Ethereum is the original road: the

00:09:06.537 --> 00:09:09.104
most secure, but with higher fees.

00:09:09.104 --> 00:09:12.000
Layer 2s are faster, cheaper roads built on top

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.894
of Ethereum, and there are several of them.

00:09:14.894 --> 00:09:17.116
Your address can exist on many roads

00:09:17.116 --> 00:09:19.218
at once, but a parcel only travels

00:09:19.218 --> 00:09:21.139
on the road it was sent on.

00:09:21.139 --> 00:09:23.217
Send on a road your wallet doesn't

00:09:23.217 --> 00:09:25.295
support, and the money can be very

00:09:25.295 --> 00:09:27.254
hard, or impossible, to get back.

00:09:28.554 --> 00:09:30.954
Every trip on those roads needs a stamp.

00:09:30.954 --> 00:09:32.299
It's called gas.

00:09:32.299 --> 00:09:35.243
When you send a transaction, you pay a small

00:09:35.243 --> 00:09:38.450
fee in the network's own coin, which on Ethereum

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:41.394
and most layer 2s is ETH. The computers that

00:09:41.394 --> 00:09:45.516
run the network, called validators, check your transaction and

00:09:45.516 --> 00:09:46.994
add it to a block.

00:09:46.994 --> 00:09:49.559
When roads are busy, stamps cost more.

00:09:49.559 --> 00:09:52.155
And one detail catches people out: if a

00:09:52.155 --> 00:09:55.529
transaction fails, the stamp is usually still spent.

00:09:56.829 --> 00:09:58.994
Here's what happened when Sam pressed send.

00:09:58.994 --> 00:10:02.419
Sam's wallet signed the transaction with their private key.

00:10:02.419 --> 00:10:03.364
It went out to the

00:10:03.364 --> 00:10:05.504
network, where validators checked it.

00:10:05.504 --> 00:10:07.239
It was sealed into a block.

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.540
And a few moments later,

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:10.193
it arrived at the address.

00:10:10.193 --> 00:10:11.988
Once it's in a block, it's final.

00:10:13.289 --> 00:10:16.179
So Sam's first transfer followed five steps.

00:10:16.179 --> 00:10:18.127
Copy the address with the wallet's

00:10:18.127 --> 00:10:19.929
copy button, never type it.

00:10:19.929 --> 00:10:22.089
Choose a network your wallet supports.

00:10:22.089 --> 00:10:23.663
Check the first and last 6

00:10:23.663 --> 00:10:25.829
characters, ideally all of them.

00:10:25.829 --> 00:10:27.886
Send a small test, about $10,

00:10:27.886 --> 00:10:29.969
and wait until it arrives.

00:10:29.969 --> 00:10:31.481
Then send the rest, and add

00:10:31.481 --> 00:10:33.994
the address to your exchange's whitelist.

00:10:33.994 --> 00:10:35.759
It took ten extra minutes.

00:10:35.759 --> 00:10:38.864
Those ten minutes are the cheapest insurance in crypto.

00:10:40.164 --> 00:10:41.254
Quick check.

00:10:41.254 --> 00:10:44.398
Sam's exchange offers two networks for a withdrawal,

00:10:44.398 --> 00:10:47.249
and Sam's wallet supports only one of them.

00:10:47.249 --> 00:10:48.294
What should Sam do?

00:10:52.714 --> 00:10:55.733
The answer: choose the network the wallet supports,

00:10:55.733 --> 00:10:57.765
send a small test, and wait for it

00:10:57.765 --> 00:10:59.739
to arrive before sending the rest.

00:11:01.039 --> 00:11:02.279
Chapter 5.

00:11:02.279 --> 00:11:03.794
Machines with no cashier.

00:11:05.094 --> 00:11:07.286
Now for the part everyone talks

00:11:07.286 --> 00:11:10.669
about: DeFi, short for decentralised finance.

00:11:10.669 --> 00:11:13.149
At its heart is the smart contract, and the

00:11:13.149 --> 00:11:15.759
best picture for it is a vending machine.

00:11:15.759 --> 00:11:17.224
You insert coins.

00:11:17.224 --> 00:11:20.314
The rules, written in code, decide what happens.

00:11:20.314 --> 00:11:22.159
You get the result instantly.

00:11:22.159 --> 00:11:23.664
And there's no cashier.

00:11:23.664 --> 00:11:25.240
If you press the wrong button,

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.014
there's no one to refund you.

00:11:27.014 --> 00:11:29.615
DeFi apps are simply collections of these

00:11:29.615 --> 00:11:32.339
machines, for swapping, lending and earning.

00:11:33.638 --> 00:11:34.838
So how do you learn to

00:11:34.838 --> 00:11:37.188
use machines that don't give refunds?

00:11:37.188 --> 00:11:39.313
The same way pilots learn to fly.

00:11:39.313 --> 00:11:43.043
Pilots train in a simulator before they carry passengers.

00:11:43.043 --> 00:11:45.146
In crypto, the simulator is a test

00:11:45.146 --> 00:11:47.429
network, where the coins are free and

00:11:47.429 --> 00:11:50.133
worth nothing, but the buttons are real.

00:11:50.133 --> 00:11:51.610
Then you do a short hop,

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:53.328
with a tiny real amount.

00:11:53.328 --> 00:11:55.233
You check it did what you expected.

00:11:55.233 --> 00:11:56.699
And only then do you fly

00:11:56.699 --> 00:11:58.985
normally, with the same careful habits.

00:12:00.286 --> 00:12:03.656
Sam's first DeFi steps followed that exact path.

00:12:03.656 --> 00:12:06.421
Free test tokens on a test network first.

00:12:06.421 --> 00:12:09.181
Connecting the wallet only from a bookmarked site,

00:12:09.181 --> 00:12:11.158
never from a link in a message.

00:12:11.158 --> 00:12:14.312
Reading every request before signing: what it does,

00:12:14.312 --> 00:12:16.371
who it goes to, and how much.

00:12:16.371 --> 00:12:17.931
And only after the test

00:12:17.931 --> 00:12:19.686
worked, one tiny real swap.

00:12:20.986 --> 00:12:22.714
One idea from this chapter

00:12:22.714 --> 00:12:24.486
is worth holding on to.

00:12:24.486 --> 00:12:27.474
In DeFi, your wallet asks you to sign things.

00:12:27.474 --> 00:12:28.949
A signature is like a signed

00:12:28.949 --> 00:12:30.674
cheque with your name on it.

00:12:30.674 --> 00:12:32.869
Read what you're signing, every time.

00:12:32.869 --> 00:12:34.924
If you don't understand it, don't sign it.

00:12:36.224 --> 00:12:37.474
Chapter 6.

00:12:37.474 --> 00:12:38.809
Where beginners get hurt.

00:12:40.109 --> 00:12:42.813
Sam's friend, the one who lost everything with

00:12:42.813 --> 00:12:45.644
one wrong click, didn't do anything unusual.

00:12:45.644 --> 00:12:47.546
Almost every beginner loss starts

00:12:47.546 --> 00:12:49.189
with one of four things.

00:12:49.189 --> 00:12:50.816
A lost seed phrase, with

00:12:50.816 --> 00:12:52.809
no backup and no recovery.

00:12:52.809 --> 00:12:54.849
Coins sent on the wrong network.

00:12:54.849 --> 00:12:56.740
A fake website or a fake support

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:58.989
agent who tricks you into signing.

00:12:58.989 --> 00:13:00.953
And rushing: urgency and fear of

00:13:00.953 --> 00:13:03.158
missing out beating good habits.

00:13:03.158 --> 00:13:05.475
Module 0 exists to close all four

00:13:05.475 --> 00:13:07.723
doors before you move real money.

00:13:09.023 --> 00:13:11.888
Scams in crypto follow patterns, and once you've

00:13:11.888 --> 00:13:14.468
seen them, they're much easier to spot.

00:13:14.468 --> 00:13:16.938
Fake sites, with look-alike web addresses,

00:13:16.938 --> 00:13:19.363
often bought as sponsored search ads.

00:13:19.363 --> 00:13:21.950
Drainers: a single signature that quietly

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.653
grants a stranger access to everything.

00:13:24.653 --> 00:13:27.281
Fake support, in direct messages you never

00:13:27.281 --> 00:13:29.965
asked for, asking for your seed phrase.

00:13:29.965 --> 00:13:33.395
And poisoned addresses: a look-alike address slipped into

00:13:33.395 --> 00:13:36.475
your history, hoping you'll copy it by mistake.

00:13:36.475 --> 00:13:38.510
The pattern is always the same.

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:41.045
Pressure, and a shortcut around your own checks.

00:13:42.345 --> 00:13:44.963
So Sam finished the module with a security

00:13:44.963 --> 00:13:48.125
baseline, four habits that prevent most losses.

00:13:48.125 --> 00:13:50.537
A hardware wallet, once the amount matters,

00:13:50.537 --> 00:13:52.865
so the keys never touch the internet.

00:13:52.865 --> 00:13:56.935
Unique passwords in a password manager, and two-factor everywhere.

00:13:56.935 --> 00:14:00.025
Never sharing keys, with anyone, for any reason.

00:14:00.025 --> 00:14:02.419
And bookmarking the apps they use, never

00:14:02.419 --> 00:14:04.755
following links to wallets or DeFi apps.

00:14:06.055 --> 00:14:07.853
And there are three rules that sit

00:14:07.853 --> 00:14:10.105
above everything else in this program.

00:14:10.105 --> 00:14:11.697
Only use money you can afford

00:14:11.697 --> 00:14:13.270
to lose while you learn.

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:16.373
Never trust anyone who promises returns, including

00:14:16.373 --> 00:14:18.620
anyone who says they're from us.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:21.070
And slow is safe: there's no deadline in

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.760
crypto that's worth a mistake you can't undo.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:26.505
Last quick check.

00:14:26.505 --> 00:14:28.290
A message says a new token will

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.019
double in an hour and asks Sam

00:14:30.019 --> 00:14:32.495
to connect their wallet through a link.

00:14:32.495 --> 00:14:33.690
What's the right move?

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:39.925
The answer: ignore it.

00:14:39.925 --> 00:14:42.299
Urgency, a promised return and a link

00:14:42.299 --> 00:14:44.598
are three warning signs at once.

00:14:44.598 --> 00:14:46.143
Only use bookmarked sites.

00:14:47.444 --> 00:14:48.684
Chapter 7.

00:14:48.684 --> 00:14:50.359
Your path through Module 0.

00:14:51.659 --> 00:14:53.944
Here's your journey, stop by stop.

00:14:53.944 --> 00:14:56.694
0.0, the Mastery Starter, where you'll see

00:14:56.694 --> 00:14:59.489
what mastery of this module looks like.

00:14:59.489 --> 00:15:03.721
0.1, money, ledgers and why blockchains exist.

00:15:03.721 --> 00:15:07.211
0.2, opening and securing an exchange.

00:15:07.211 --> 00:15:10.651
0.3, your first buy without overpaying.

00:15:10.651 --> 00:15:13.226
0.4, who holds the keys.

00:15:13.226 --> 00:15:16.681
0.5, setting up and backing up your wallet.

00:15:16.681 --> 00:15:20.516
0.6, networks, gas and your first transfer.

00:15:20.516 --> 00:15:24.856
0.7, your first DeFi steps, practice mode first.

00:15:24.856 --> 00:15:27.240
And 0.8, your security baseline,

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:29.046
and the language of DeFi.

00:15:30.345 --> 00:15:33.078
Underneath those lessons are seven practical

00:15:33.078 --> 00:15:35.200
steps, the same ones Sam took.

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:36.210
Accounts.

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:37.440
The exchange.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:38.670
A first buy.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:39.700
The wallet.

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:41.135
A first transfer.

00:15:41.135 --> 00:15:42.520
Practice DeFi.

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:44.325
And your security baseline.

00:15:44.325 --> 00:15:47.125
Do them in order, with a small learning amount.

00:15:47.125 --> 00:15:48.416
Allow 2 to 3 hours,

00:15:48.416 --> 00:15:50.200
spread over a few days.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:51.165
There's no rush.

00:15:52.465 --> 00:15:55.030
And you won't have to remember any of it.

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:57.421
Everything is in the Day-1 Setup Kit, a

00:15:57.421 --> 00:16:00.470
printable checklist in the Start here chapter.

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:02.310
Tick each box as you go.

00:16:02.310 --> 00:16:04.845
When every box is ticked, you're set up safely.

00:16:06.145 --> 00:16:08.660
Here's how Sam's first week actually went.

00:16:08.660 --> 00:16:10.559
Day 1: a password manager, a

00:16:10.559 --> 00:16:13.805
secured email, and two-factor authentication.

00:16:13.805 --> 00:16:17.100
Day 2: the exchange account opened and locked down.

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.568
Day 3: a small first buy, and the wallet

00:16:19.568 --> 00:16:22.275
set up with the backup written on paper.

00:16:22.275 --> 00:16:24.000
Day 4: a restore test, then

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.640
the first transfer, test amount first.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.262
Day 5: practice on a test network, one

00:16:29.262 --> 00:16:32.385
tiny real swap, and the security baseline.

00:16:32.385 --> 00:16:34.030
About half an hour a day.

00:16:34.030 --> 00:16:35.105
Nothing heroic.

00:16:36.405 --> 00:16:38.380
Every lesson works the same way.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:39.700
Watch the video.

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:40.940
Read the lesson.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.895
Do the checklist, for real, with small amounts.

00:16:43.895 --> 00:16:46.440
Then take the short quiz before moving on.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:47.703
That rhythm is how you

00:16:47.703 --> 00:16:49.515
build habits, not just knowledge.

00:16:50.815 --> 00:16:52.860
So how did Sam's story end?

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:55.467
A week later, Sam had a secured exchange

00:16:55.467 --> 00:16:58.709
account, a wallet they controlled, a backup they'd

00:16:58.709 --> 00:17:01.888
tested, a first transfer that arrived safely, and

00:17:01.888 --> 00:17:04.350
a first practice DeFi transaction.

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:05.640
Nothing lost.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:06.895
Nothing rushed.

00:17:06.895 --> 00:17:08.800
And for the first time, the words

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:10.592
on the news actually made sense.

00:17:11.892 --> 00:17:13.055
Sam didn't need to be

00:17:13.055 --> 00:17:14.942
technical, and neither do you.

00:17:14.942 --> 00:17:16.552
You just need to go in order,

00:17:16.552 --> 00:17:19.267
keep amounts small, and follow the checklist.

00:17:19.267 --> 00:17:22.019
This is education, not financial advice,

00:17:22.019 --> 00:17:24.332
and no results are guaranteed.

00:17:24.332 --> 00:17:25.791
What you'll get is a safe

00:17:25.791 --> 00:17:28.092
foundation, and a process you understand.

00:17:29.392 --> 00:17:31.487
That's the story of Module 0.

00:17:31.487 --> 00:17:32.882
Now it's your turn.

